window.__labLoaded({"id":"3.4","title":"The Normal Distribution","goal":"Use the heights of adult men (mean 69.1 in, SD 2.9 in) to find the share shorter than 66 inches and the height of the 90th percentile.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Describe the bell curve","text":"A normal distribution is fixed by two numbers: its centre (the mean) and its width (the standard deviation). SciPy bundles both into one object we can ask questions of.","code":"from scipy import stats\n\nmean, sd = 69.1, 2.9\nheights = stats.norm(loc=mean, scale=sd)\n\nprint('median =', heights.median())","out":"median = 69.1","figs":[]},{"title":"Share shorter than 66 inches","text":"cdf gives the area under the curve to the left of a value, which is the probability of being at or below it. The z-score says how many standard deviations 66 is below the mean.","code":"z = (66 - mean) / sd\nshare = heights.cdf(66)\n\nprint(f'z = {z:.2f}')\nprint(f'P(height < 66) = {share:.4f}')","out":"z = -1.07\nP(height < 66) = 0.1425","figs":[]},{"title":"The 90th percentile","text":"ppf runs the other way: give it an area and it returns the value. Only 10% of men are taller than the height it prints.","code":"p90 = heights.ppf(0.90)\n\nprint(f'90th percentile = {p90:.1f} inches')","out":"90th percentile = 72.8 inches","figs":[]},{"title":"Check the 68-95-99.7 rule","text":"The empirical rule says about 68%, 95% and 99.7% of values lie within 1, 2 and 3 standard deviations of the mean. We check it with the standard normal curve.","code":"for k in (1, 2, 3):\n    inside = stats.norm.cdf(k) - stats.norm.cdf(-k)\n    print(f'within {k} SD: {inside:.4f}')","out":"within 1 SD: 0.6827\nwithin 2 SD: 0.9545\nwithin 3 SD: 0.9973","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw it","text":"The shaded area is the probability we computed for men shorter than 66 inches. A picture makes it clear that probabilities are areas.","code":"import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nx = np.linspace(mean - 4 * sd, mean + 4 * sd, 300)\nplt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.plot(x, heights.pdf(x))\nplt.fill_between(x, heights.pdf(x), where=x < 66, alpha=0.4)\nplt.xlabel('height (inches)')\nplt.show()","out":"","figs":["3.4-5.png"]}]},"r":{"code":"pnorm(66, mean = 69.1, sd = 2.9)\nqnorm(0.90, mean = 69.1, sd = 2.9)\npnorm(1) - pnorm(-1)\npnorm(2) - pnorm(-2)","expect":"0.1425; 72.82; 0.6827; 0.9545","note":"pnorm gives the area to the left of a value, and qnorm is its inverse."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (use the CDF.NORMAL and IDF.NORMAL functions)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / dummy.\nBEGIN DATA\n1\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE p_below_66 = CDF.NORMAL(66, 69.1, 2.9).\nCOMPUTE height_p90 = IDF.NORMAL(0.90, 69.1, 2.9).\nCOMPUTE within_1sd = CDF.NORMAL(1, 0, 1) - CDF.NORMAL(-1, 0, 1).\nFORMATS p_below_66 height_p90 within_1sd (F8.4).\nLIST p_below_66 height_p90 within_1sd.","note":"CDF.NORMAL(value, mean, sd) is the area to the left; IDF.NORMAL(area, mean, sd) is the inverse."},"stata":{"code":"display normal((66 - 69.1) / 2.9)\ndisplay 69.1 + 2.9 * invnormal(0.90)\ndisplay normal(1) - normal(-1)","note":"normal(z) is the area to the left of a z-score and invnormal(p) is its inverse, so convert to and from z by hand."},"try":["Change the cut-off to 72 inches and predict whether the share below it is above or below 50%, then run it.","Find the heights that mark the middle 95% of men with heights.ppf(0.025) and heights.ppf(0.975). How do they compare with mean plus or minus 2 SD?"]});
